<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nizami Ganjavi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1141–<a href="/years/1209">1209</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Nizami</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nizami Ganjavi was a Persian poet from Ganja in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, regarded as the greatest master of the romantic epic in Persian. His Khamsa (Five Treasures) includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/layla-and-majnun">Layla and Majnun</a>, dedicated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhsitan-i">Akhsitan I</a>, and Khosrow and Shirin, and he enjoyed the patronage of the Eldiguzid atabegs. His work was widely imitated across the Persian, Turkic and Indian worlds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi.jpg" alt="Nizami Ganjavi" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Behzad, 1482 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nezami_(detail)_Addressing_his_Son_Mohammad._Attributed_to_Behzad,_Leyli_and_Majnun_(Nizami_Ganjavi)_1482.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nizami Ganjavi</b>’s Layla and Majnun, one of the most retold love stories of the Islamic world, was composed in 1188 at the request of Shirvanshah Akhsitan I.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Omar Khayyam, also a great mathematician and astronomer, wrote quatrains on the brevity of life; <b>Nizami</b> wrote romantic epics such as Layla and Majnun and Khosrow and Shirin; Attar wrote the Conference of the Birds, an allegory of the mystical path; Saadi wrote the Gulistan, “The Rose Garden”,…</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2002 the minister Motiur Rahman <b>Nizami</b> proposed changing its words, which the Cabinet Division did not accept.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a></li><li><blockquote>…Hussain Khan Bandanawazi, Farid Ayaz and Abu Muhammad Qawwal, Jafar Hussain Khan, the sitar and sarod players Ustad Vilayat Khan and Ustad Shujaat Khan, Zila Khan, the <b>Nizami</b> Bandhu, Hadiqa Kiani, Smita Bellur, Lata Mangeshkar, Asha Bhosle, Rahat Fateh Ali Khan, Kailash Kher, Kavita Seth and Maithili Thakur.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chhaap-tilak-sab-chheeni">Chhaap Tilak Sab Chheeni</a></li><li><blockquote>Their court patronised Persian poetry, including <b>Nizami Ganjavi</b>, and the architect Ajami Nakhchivani built the Momine Khatun mausoleum in Nakhchivan in 1186.</blockquote><a href="/state/eldiguzids">Eldiguzids</a></li><li><blockquote>Fakhr al-Din Bahramshah of Erzincan, who ruled for some sixty years, received the dedication of <b>Nizami's</b> poem Makhzan al-Asrar.</blockquote><a href="/state/mengujekids">Mengujekids</a></li></ul></section>
